9. Mythos

The whole of mythology could be taken as a sort of projection of the collective unconscious.
(Carl Jung, Symbols of Transformation)

Prometheus matched himself in wit with the almighty son of Cronus. For when the gods and mortal men had a dispute at Mecone, even then Prometheus was forward to cut up a great ox and set portions before them, trying to deceive the mind of Zeus. Before the rest he set flesh and inner parts thick with fat upon the hide, covering them with an ox paunch; but for Zeus he put the white bones dressed up with cunning art and covered with shining fat.
(Hesiod, Theogony)

Humans are great storytellers. Some of our best and oldest stories are Myths. These types of stories have entertained, inspired, and even rooted tradition for millennia. Once we started talking we just couldn't help ourselves from offering up fantastical accounts of the world (probably due to pent-up archetypes and ideas yearning to be released). The fact that many myths, despite being ridiculous, have survived this long is miraculous. But we love them and think they are very important—whether they are true or not. Why? I speculate it is because myths capture something foundational about the human experience. They hold ancient knowledge and tap into the parley of the Mind (the best myths connecting us to the Collective Unconscious).    
	Myths can teach us a lot, if we are willing to look closely, and apply some philosophical methodology. Take the trick at Mecone. The story, usually, is taken to be an explanation for a particular custom: animal sacrifice. But we might ask ourself, why does Prometheus care so much; that he is willing to risk the wrath of Zeus? Prometheus is not a lover of animals, but a lover of man; and we can see this again when he brings Fire to humans—ultimately to his demise. If we take the myth, in a more serious way, we can see that what is being negotiated is not an ox sacrifice, but the sacrifice of man. Prometheus’ trick is to offer Zeus glamoured up bones; that is our soul after death. The thing he wants to keep is our body, ugly on the outside, but rich inside. The wit, wisdom, craft of Prometheus, his clever trick: there is no life after death; bones are bones, and Humans gain everything in this life. Zeus would eventually discover the deception and demand both our bodies and souls as payback—the cost of fire: that humans must give up everything to the gods.
	Another myth that we tend not to take too seriously is the deluge destruction story, found in nearly every culture. We either take it metaphoric of man's rebirth from sin, or as some real, localized Big Flood that happened thousands of years ago. But if we readjust our sights, it should occur to us that the story being told is one of the Mind. The deluge, in my view, is the flooding of the unconscious by the Conscious; a burying of all the things old, to make way for the new. Even Sigmund Freud's glacier model has the unconscious buried beneath water. The significance of this interpretation is that the storyteller, pushing the metaphor, is likely the Unconscious; the one providing inspiration and poetic art. It has reason to tell its story, in that it has been wronged. 
	The story of Eve, and her encounter with the Serpent at the Tree of Wisdom, also has something worthwhile to teach. The first thing to pay attention to is exploitation of archetypes. The image of the Serpent arouses fear, suspicion, mystery, mischief. The image of the Fruit evokes appetite, desire, lust, temptation. Eve is supposedly lured by the Serpent to eat the fruit from the tree. But there is nothing in the story that explicitly points to manipulation on the part of the Serpent. Instead, the Serpent simply tells Eve that no harm will come to her if she eats the fruit. And unlike the lie that God tells (that they will surely die if they eat from the tree) this seems to be true. Adam and Eve leave the Garden on their own accord. God does not force them out. How would He? Unless God took over Adam and Eve's minds; Or threatened to kill them? But there is no indication in the story that this is the case (barring the cherubim preventing reentry). Instead, Adam and Eve leave because they feel guilt for betraying God. But notice that the Serpent’s words still hold. No harm comes to them. It is instead their own minds which condemn them.
	God's Lie is worth looking at. Why would God lie about the Tree of Wisdom? It seems unlikely that it would be out of malice or spite. Perhaps control? But why allow such a tree at all? One way to make it coherent is take the story at face value. God was in fact telling the truth and the Serpent was lying. After eating the Fruit, Adam and Eve do in fact die in some sense. Their innocence dies. They become cursed beings. But cursed how? I take it the curse is Consciousness itself—being what knowledge of good and evil is supposed to signify. For Eden, we might take the archetype of the Garden as representing Nature. Adam and Eve initially live in harmony with nature in a happy blissful state. It is apparent that Adam and Eve, which I take to be representative of some archaic hominid species, or perhaps early humans, are Unconscious for some time. It is only after eating the forbidden fruit that they realize they are Naked. In shame they cover themselves—symbolizing Consciousness' arrival. When this event occurred is likely fluid, but it was a pivotal moment in the history of this planet. An animal, donning clothes, rose above nature and claimed it as his own, aware of his own superiority, and suffering. Never to be the same! 
	Besides having knowledge of good and evil, the Serpent tells Eve she will become like God by eating from the tree. What does this mean? Take it from a different angle. It is possible that eating the fruit created God in the mind. That is, consciousness itself will always produce God, or some type of religious thinking. So, in a sense, God did drive Adam and Eve out of the Garden. Though it is not original sin, but rather theological guilt which we harbor inside of us. Notably, going deeper, it is quite possible the Serpent was not trying to persuade Eve to eat the fruit but rather dissuade her. We need to pay attention to our archetypes. In nearly all myths containing the serpent archetype the Serpent is hostile and there to scare you away. Why in this special case is the Serpent friendly, giving, openly offering what it is supposed to be guarding. It doesn’t add up. Instead, I take it, the Serpent is there to prevent Us from becoming Conscious. It is Nature's attempt to preserve the sanctity of the natural world. A world incompatible with god-like dominance. The implication being that developing Consciousness was a bad thing for our ancestors and has been a source of misery ever since. (Including the burden of carrying God in our minds forever.)
	We might also go back to the inception, to Father Time and the void. The early Greek mythos seems to largely center around incestuous families and their many quarrels. Brothers and sisters, mothers and sons, fathers and daughters, marrying each other and giving birth to offspring radically different from themselves; the new generation always to overthrow the old; betrayal, infanticide, mutilation, patricide, wars, you name it. This may be read as high scandal. But even for the Ancient Greek ear it was far-fetched—for people anyway. However, if the early mythos is read not as a story of people but as a story of ideas and archetypes it makes much more sense.
	There are varying cosmogonies in the Greek tradition.  But most agree to something like the following. In the very beginning there was Chronos (Time) and Chaos or Nothing. They produced the cosmic egg, which hatched Phanes (Day) and Nyx (Night). Here, I think, the cosmic egg could be interpreted as the mind, with Day as consciousness and Night as unconsciousness. In this sense, the world didn't truly exist until perceived by a mind. Phanes and Nyx gave birth to Gaia (Earth), Uranus (The Heavens), and Eros (Love). I interpret Earth to represent nature, The Heavens to represent non-material reality outside of nature, or the non-physical, and Love to represent emotion. The mingling of these Three first lead to the birth of monstrosities, like the Cyclopes and Hecatoncheires.  These first gods I take to be archaic archetypes. Eventually Gaia gave birth to gods with human-like features, being the twelve original Titans: Oceanus, Themis, Rhea, Coeus, Crius, Tethys, Phoebe, Hyperion, Lapetus, Theia, Mnemosyne, and Cronus (not to be confused with Chronos). These Titans I take to be representative of the more advanced anthropomorphic archetypes. Here we have what might be viewed as the First World Order. 
	Uranus turned out to be a terrible father and hated his children. He hated the Cyclopes and Hecatoncheires in particular, and banished them to Tartarus, the deepest pits beneath the earth, far deeper than Hades. I take Tartarus to be a rather clear representation of the deep unconscious. In this sense, the archaic archetypes become repressed and forgotten. Relinquishing some of her children, more correctly her first children, made Gaia very upset. In retaliation, Gaia solicited the help of her other children, the twelve Titans, to overthrow Uranus and free her first children. Only the youngest Titan, Cronus, offered to aid his mother. Gaia gave Cronus a powerful sickle which he used to castrate Uranus as he attempted to lay with her. In his mutilation Uranus fled and disappeared. With Uranus deposed Cronus became the ruler of the world. The castration of Uranus has symbolic significance. By being castrated Uranus no longer is able to reproduce or create new lineages.  This permanently severs the link to the Heavens, or non-material reality. Cronus and the Titans start a new line that is much more Earth-bound. But Cronus does absorb some his father's power, and inherits his non-physical aspects. I take Cronus to represent the primitive idea of God. And from this lineage will sprout more sophisticated ideas, embodied by his children, the Olympian gods. 
	Upon gaining power, Cronus fulfills his mother's wish and frees the Cyclopes and Hecatoncheires from Tartarus. He marries his sister Rhea, and with the rest of the Titans rules over the world. This might be viewed as the Second World Order. It is suggested that Cronus ruled peacefully for a very long time. For instance, in antiquity, rule under Cronus is described as a golden age for mankind, or at least for the first men —which might be interpreted as early humans. But eventually Rhea began to produce offspring. Whether it be from Gaia, or whispers from the lingering Uranus, Cronus hears the prophecy that one of his children will depose him as he deposed his own father. This makes Cronus very paranoid. He rebanishes the Cyclopes and Hecatoncheires, and swallows his children as Rhea gives birth to them. In desperation, Rhea secretly replaced her last child, Zeus, with a stone (the Omphalos stone), feeding it to Cronus in his place. Zeus is then hidden away outside of Cronus' reach. Something worth mentioning is that paranoia of being overthrown is a persistent characteristic of the Godhead in all forms taken. There will always be hostility toward rival ideas, and depending on the form, this extends to other ideas in general. The true desire of God is to be the only idea that occupies the mind.  
	After Zeus matures, he and his mother seek out the help of Metis, Zeus' older cousin and the first goddess of wisdom, to save the rest of the Olympian gods. Metis crafts a special emetic to give to Cronus. Disguised as a cupbearer Zeus feeds the emetic to Cronus causing him to regurgitate the swallowed gods: Poseidon, Hades, Demeter, Hera, and Hestia. Upon seeing his children freed Cronus flees. In gratitude, the emancipated gods make Zeus their leader. This then leads to a brutal war between the Titans and the newly freed Olympian gods. Something significant to look at is how the Olympian gods were freed. With the aid of Metis' craft, they escape out of Cronus' mouth. I take this to be representative of the evolution of language. Speech allows the new gods (ideas) to escape out of the mouth. In this way, ideas can become much more sophisticated and elaborate. Instead of a basic form or model; with the aid of language, we can, for instance, using the later Apollo, give him a bow—to represent archery—and a lyre–to represent music and the arts—give him the power of healing and prophesy, make him watch over herders, associate him with the Sun, and so on. This allows for the creation of very powerful and captivating deities. 
	With the assistance of Metis, a few Titan defectors, and the Cyclopes and Hecatoncheires (newly freed by Zeus) , the Olympians win the war against Cronus and the Titans. Zeus then banishes the Titans to Tartarus. (My interpretation here is that ideas (Olympians) replace the anthropomorphic archetypes (Titans) and push them to the deep unconscious (Tartarus).) With the Titans gone, Zeus and the other Olympians (to grow to twelve) rule over the world. This establishes the Third World Order: the reign of Ideas. More wars will follow, against the Giants (lingering products of Uranus) and Gaia's monstrous offspring Typhon (which I take to be the embodiment of all the Archaic Archetypes), but the Olympians prevail, eventually receiving Gaia's blessing—or her acknowledgment of defeat. This signifies the Age of Man and his conquest and exploitation of the Earth. Of course, I should acknowledge, the Greek Pantheon, and polytheism, will eventually be replaced by a monotheistic Judeo-Christian god.  But I don't necessarily see this as a new world order, as far as the Mind is concerned. The Judeo-Christian god, in his simplicity, is more like Cronus than Zeus in my view. Perhaps it is a return to the Second World Order: rule by a single primitive Idea and all His anthropomorphic archetypes (reflected in the decorations and mythos of the Church). 
	Another important theme in the Mythos that tends to be reoccurring is immortality. There are various symbols capturing it like Ambrosia/Nectar, The Fountain(s) of Youth, Magic Elixirs, The Tree of Life, The Holy Grail, Blessed/Cursed Blood, and so forth. In most traditions, gods also tend to be immortal. So becoming immortal is linked to achieving godliness. An interesting spin on the immortality tale comes from one of the oldest myths, or at least one of the oldest myths written down and (mostly) preserved: the Sumerian Legend of Adapa. The story starts right after the Great Flood. The human race is left without guidance, so Ea, god of wisdom, decides to bestow perfect intelligence (and wisdom) on a mortal man, Adapa.  It's not exactly clear from the text how Adapa uses this intelligence, but scholars typically take him to have become/be a great sage or king. One day while out on the water fishing Adapa's boat is capsized by a strong gust of wind. Adapa becomes infuriated by this and in his anger breaks the wings of the South Wind. As a result, the South Wind does not blow for seven days.
	The South Wind not blowing catches the attention of the powerful sky god, Anu. He summons Adapa to the heavens to answer for the transgression. Being concerned, Ea counsels Adapa on how to handle himself in Anu's court. Ea first gives advice on how to flatter the old gods Tammuz and Gishida, the doorkeepers to Anu—by wearing mourning clothes and acknowledging them as being both dead and revived. He next tells Adapa not to accept the food and drink in heaven if offered, warning Adapa that he will die if he eats or drinks from these sources. This turns out to be a lie. (Compare this to the lie that God tells Adam and Eve in Eden.) Finally, Ea tells Adapa to anoint himself in oil and heavenly garments after meeting Anu.
	Adapa does what he is told. He flatters the doorkeepers and makes it to Anu. But Anu has a change of heart and is no longer angry at Adapa. He thinks it cruel that a being should possess intelligence without immortality. So he offers Adapa the food and water of life, which will make him immortal. In loyalty and trust to Ea, Adapa refuses the offer. This highly insults Anu. Anu allows Adapa to anoint himself with oil and heavenly garments; but condemns him and the rest of mankind to be forever mortal. Immortality always to be out of reach. (Also compare to the clothing event in Genesis. Adapa, like Adam and Eve, dress and pretend to be gods, even though they clearly are not; as immortality is stripped from them.)
	There are a number of things to pull from the Adapa legend. Foremost, why did Ea intentionally sabotage Adapa to prevent him from becoming immortal? Was it because he knew having both intelligence and immortality would make Adapa a god? Was Ea protecting the gods’ sacred divine status? But this doesn’t necessarily make sense. Why would Ea give Adapa intelligence in the first place? And Anu seems to have no problem granting godhood to Adapa. Perhaps, instead, Ea was benevolent, knowing that immortality would be bad for Adapa and mankind. In this sense, he was protecting Adapa. There seems to be a theme, and we also see it in the Sumerian Epic of Gilgamesh; that immortality is not something intended for mankind, and we should give up its pursuit. That humans are better off mortal.
	Something that stands out in the myths is that neither knowledge/intelligence nor immortality in themselves are taken to be bad in isolation. It is only in combination that they are bad. For instance, God is content with Adam and Eve eating from the Tree of Life and being immortal beings. It is only this immortality in combination with knowledge of good and evil, or intelligence, that He forbids. Likewise, Ea is content with Adapa having intelligence, even perfect intelligence. It is only intelligence in combination with immortality that he forbids. Perhaps there is something to this. Mortality and death (along with intelligence) are defining features of human existence. If we ever did consume the food and water of life, and achieve immortality—which is a real possibility with scientific advancement—we may lose out on what it means to be human. The world, and Nature, aren’t ready for humans becoming gods. Such power may be the end of everything.  


10. Sipping the Mead of Poetic Inspiration

The greatest of goods come to us through madness, provided it is bestowed by divine gift.
(Plato, Phaedrus)

Kvasir went up and down the earth to give instruction to men; and when he came upon invitation to the abode of certain dwarves, Fjalar and Galarr, they called him into privy converse with them, and killed him, letting his blood run into two vats and a kettle. The kettle is named Ódrerir, and the vats Són and Bodn; they blended honey with the blood, and the outcome was that mead by the virtue of which he who drinks becomes a skald or scholar.
(Snorri Sturluson, Skáldskaparmál)

In Norse mythology the Mead of Poetic Inspiration was said to grant godlike prowess in the Arts. It sometimes is described as being possessed by Odin—in which he temporarily gifts his poetic expertise to those who call. Drinking the Mead, in this tradition, is not to be taken lightly on account of it causing a crazed and frenzied state in those under its influence. Otherworldly inspiration can also be found in Greek mythology, as the gods help humans in their intellectual pursuits, most notably the Muses, who assist artists in the act of creating superb works. This is most profound in poetry in which it seems to most of all tap into a divine source. For example, Socrates, in his evaluation, held that poets compose poems, not from knowledge, but instead from divine inspiration, even madness. 
	Poetic madness, so to speak, is also found outside of the Nordic and Greek traditions and is well-documented in modernity. Usually in the contemporary context, claims of divine inspiration or poetic madness are typically treated as aspects of mental illness. There may well be a strong link. But I am concerned more with divine inspiration or poetic madness, if this is possible, that comes in waves, temporarily inspiring an individual to create superb works (rather than full-blown or permanent psychosis which hinders rather than helps the creative process and prevents one from being an artist in any practical sense). This type of inspiration need not come from God or gods or supernatural forces but rather involves tapping into the Unconscious, which has a mystical quality to it (and the emotional impact of art simply exploits the archetypes and ancestral memories, or more generally the Collective Unconscious). 
	The Poet and Poetry is the focus of this section because he/it is taken to be engulfed (emotionally) by the creative process; and because myths, which was discussed in the previous section, and will be hinted at throughout, are poetic in origin. Showing some bias, I ask whether the Philosopher does something similar to the Poet, and how we might exploit the same divine tap (stealing some Mead). Thus. Paying homage to the traditions of old I now call upon the Muses:

To the Muses, nine in number, the source of inspiration that the Artist draws from. To whom we pay tribute. Calliope show us your craft. Clio show us your craft. Euterpe show us your craft. Melpomene show us you craft. Terpsichore show us your craft. Erato show us your craft. Polymnia show us your craft. Urania show us your craft. Thalia show us your craft. Act as an intermediary to the frightful place where mortals dare not venture. Where Beauty is closely guarded. Take a small piece and give it to me.

Nothing. No sign. No Sister will sponsor a Philosopher. But am I not an artist like the rest? Not of words, or chords, or marble, or dance; But of ideas—An Idea Artist. Should not this be rewarded? 

Let us not forget the Muses deal in memory and forgetfulness. Philosophy came too late, and has no rhyme; let it cease and be forgotten. 

Lose one eye to Hear. Lose another to Feel. Madness will leave you blind if you seek Truth over Verse.

This is the fee. To be paid for the crime of Knowing. That you're only free to deal in beautiful fictions—if you repent. 

So, the Poets are liars? Peddlers of emotion. The most ancient art.

"Yes," a soft voice answers. 

Then the Philosopher must be a comedian. We must all agree. To do our work, we must feed shit to flies.

"Such is art," a soft voice answers.

Such a thing would not be completely Despised. But there must be more to the Poetic Art than this. Was it not a device for remembering the wisdoms of Old? Answer comely Muse.

"Yes. But not for long, I'm afraid. There was a price. Emotion will always find its way into Song, and set its own Tunes," a soft voice answers. 

Then Sing for me. It will soothe my deep wounds. Sing comely Muse. 

"Bold you are on Insisting. I must warn that you still seek the Truth," a soft voice answers. 

Then tell me what is False. Tell me all that is wrong. I promise I will do what I am told. 

"Then you seek the Oracle. Not Me. There you will find riddles disguised as Mysteries. I can only bring agony and despair. That is who I am," a soft voice answers. 

Do I care! I know you are an imposter—too prudish to be a god. 

"Then why do you listen?" a soft voice answers.

Because I am an imposter too. I am no artist. I seek only to steal from Otherworldly Sources. 

"In this there is great Wealth," a soft voice answers.

Then tell me something of Worth. Anything will suffice.

"I offer Nothing of value. You must steal from Yourself," a soft voice answers.

What even is there to take? From such a Shallow Mind.

"That is your answer," a soft voice answers.

Oh! I see my vice clearly now. There need not be harmony in rhyme. We need only to rip out the pattern from its ugly pomp. 

Words lay dormant. There is no great answer. The philosopher is a butcher. What is your name? You must tell me!

"My name is Death," a soft voice answers. 

I always knew. Have not shame. This is the beginning of Philosophy. To laugh at the absurd. To set ourselves on fire in order to rise from the ashes of doubt. To become a pitiful Bird of Prey. If only there were gods to listen!

—The Poet claims praise for this Mischief. 

—The Philosopher views it as Theft.


11. Aesthetic Culture

Culture is, before all things, the unity of artistic style, in every expression of the life of a people. 
(Friedrich Nietzsche, Thus Spoke Zarathustra)  

The Absolute is the eternal becoming, and art is the moment where this becoming pauses to reveal itself.
(Friedrich Schelling, The Ages of the World)

Culture includes many things. The most basic is language. Without language whatever culture a people may have will remain extremely primitive and be at a high risk of disappearing every new generation. But once this functional component is present the floodgates open wide. We might take culture to be beliefs, values, rituals, behaviors, tools, music, dress, exhibits, cuisine, customs, theories, etiquette, norms, poetry, celebrations, laws, prayers, sacrifices. But we don't want to say every unique thing humans do is culture. (Though, culture could be described as modes of life.) Instead, I am after the zeitgeist, essence; the spirit of culture as it thrives. We don't simply live within a particular society; we absorb it, make it our own, integrate it into our identity as something greater. I follow the traditional model of categorizing cultural movements and/or manifestations into the three categories of Art, Religion, and Philosophy. I would also add Science to the list, and many would additionally add Politics. I will focus primarily on art or aesthetic culture and touch on scientific culture here. I do this because I will spend considerable space on religion and philosophy in the sections that follow. I ignore politics mainly because it draws no useful connections to my project. Though, I admit, many see politics as the most exciting of the categories mentioned. But I would have to disagree, seeing it as material in nature rather than a subject of spirit or mind. 
	What is aesthetic culture? Art in its most primitive is a combination of memory, or immediate sense experience, and feeling. This together forming an aesthetic experience. However, there seems to be something more in play, at least for very sophisticated types of art. One answer is that art entails capturing the universal in the concrete.  But this doesn’t quite seem right, for true art as such. This would entail that art should concern itself with ideas. But I take it ideas are not the stuff of art. Though there does seem to be a type of dance between the universal and concrete happening when it comes to art. In fact, art seems to be both universal and concrete at the same time. Fortunately, in my flavor of philosophy, there is something we can point to that seems to delicately lie in the space between universality and concreteness: Archetypes. In some of the previous sections I suggested archetypes were providing poetic and artistic content. While not all art needs to be drawn from or concern archetypes, I take it archetypes are especially suited to be the stuff of art and aesthetic culture. 
	Firstly, archetypes are strongly emotion-laden. Because art in the usual sense will need to arouse emotions to function as such, as we typically take it, archetypes are particularly suited to the task. It is also the case that archetypes tend to be merged with its associative emotion(s), so it can create awe and sublimity in its effects as people unsuccessfully try to somehow separate the feeling from the universal pattern.  Archetypes are also present in all humans, so if we are to create a zeitgeist or aesthetic culture that is shared by all, this is an effective means to do so. For instance, the aesthetic sentiments of a single person or a few people would not be an aesthetic culture. A large portion of the population, or all of the population, must share the same aesthetic sentiments for it to constitute aesthetic culture. Archetypes are unique in that they allow for similar sentiments or even identical sentiments in a large number of people, uniting the culture and creating a reliable reference point in drawing a shared aesthetic or meaningful cultural experience. An additional reason in favor of the archetypal interpretation of aesthetic culture is that archetypes have familiar historical properties. Everyone, for instance, has reverence towards the Mother archetype on account of it being part of our history as far back as anyone can remember or might be recorded. There is a warm feeling towards it because it has always been there with us. It likely also made an early appearance in our childhood fables, mythology, stories, etc., leading to a special type of nostalgia as we come to age.
	But there do seem to be unique types of culture, or aesthetic culture, that must be actively maintained and defended against deterioration and infiltration. I will take this unique cultural brand, as opposed to the general or universal aesthetic, to be what’s captured by the zeitgeist or spirit of the times. I take this to be what Nietzsche means by a culture's "artistic style." And in this sense the zeitgeist takes on greater meaning and elevates individuals more so than can be explained by the collection of historical archetypes alone. What is going on? Defenders of something like a dynamic Absolute—a philosophical term of art ؟—take cultural change to result from a rational process.  But I take it, while it does follow an evolutionary process, there is nothing rational about it at all. If I might temporally run with the term, the Absolutes are in fact archetypes, not glorified ideas, or any of the other odd things the later German idealists might call it. But these Absolutes are not the same universally established archetypes we have been talking about thus far. I take it they are more like proto-archetypes, that have yet to be permanently ingrained in the human brain. 	Given a very long period of time, if a culture remained static, and the same cultural patterns continued to repeat, these proto-archetypes would eventually become permanent archetypes. But ever since we started talking and improving our societies they’ve been in flux, and moving fast. Though they do occasionally pause, long enough for us to notice, as Schelling points out, and this might be what a particular Age is if we’re able to see it as we live through it. This may sound base, but in our current historical circumstances trying to maintain or preserve a particular culture is a fool’s errand. Evolutionary ingrainment just takes far too long. But it is the aspiration of the prototypes or Absolutes to achieve permanent status. And perhaps that is what the end of history is, just things staying stable long enough for proto-archetypes to become permanent archetypes. 
	All that being said, people tend to vehemently defend their unique culture and try to maintain it. Because archetypes do in fact have causal powers, there is likely impetus for archetypes to influence behavior in a way to ensure their own preservation. If this wasn’t the case the archetypes wouldn’t have come to exist in the first place. And as I pointed out earlier, the old archetypes still exert a strong influence on our lives. It is no different for the proto-archetypes. It is just that competition is much more fierce because there are more of them and they have much more to lose—in most cases extinction. They also are not maintained naturally but need to be preserved with force, through tradition, education, laws, cultural preservation programs, and so on. Thus proto-archetypes, and hence culture, is a fragile thing.
	In many cases, culture is maintained through momentum. People prefer to hang on to the past, or what was passed down to them. But aesthetic culture, if we are to distinguish it from culture simpliciter, is a little different. In a sea of proto-archetypes to draw from, there is a choice that might be made, to select for cultural features that are the most beautiful or inspiring or useful. There are cultural movements that seek to escape the past rather than preserve it. Try something new, if you will. This is a grand feat because it gives us a chance to see, feel, know something novel and distinguish ourselves from the past and make new history. And novelty is something that tends to get remembered and studied. I will not go into the role that unique people, places, events, play in the flux of cultural change. These things do matter. But I’ll leave it at that. Though I hope this is a satisfactory explanation of aesthetic culture and the zeitgeist. 
	We can now move on to scientific culture. This is a much simpler undertaken. Scientific culture does involve ideas. Scientific culture also moves, but in principle is much more stable, or at least there is much more hope in it reaching equilibrium. It seems to be very dynamic, but this may just be because we happen to live in a particularly disruptive scientific age. Let’s not forget that many of Aristotle’s scientific theories were accepted and remained unchanged for a thousand years. This is a philosophical rather than a scientific or historical essay so I will not go into any detail here about the nature of science itself, or its practice, or its relation to technology. Though I will treat technology as a type of artifact and respond to some of its dangers later; as well as look at science’s relation to philosophy. But let us limit ourselves to scientific culture. I take scientific culture to be very broad, and hold that it need not be limited to theories produced by a/the scientific method. Rather, scientific culture captures any abstract idea that says something about the external world. When there is consensus by most, or all people, about some world-explaining idea, which we could then call a theory or law if sufficiently complex, that is the scientific culture of that age. For example, the scientific culture of the current age, for most places, contains the ideas of a plate tectonics, a heliocentric orbit, and Darwinian natural selection. None of this was maintained in previous scientific ages. Of course, not everyone believes this, and hence would be part of a different scientific culture. For instance, a geocentric orbit, or intelligent design to account for species complexity. 
	One issue is that reasonably intelligent people, including scientists, might mix competing frameworks. For instance, they may accept a heliocentric orbit along with intelligent design. In determining what the prevailing scientific culture of the age should be it gets tricky, but we might point to the theory that is more forcefully defended and maintained as representing it. For instance, Einstein’s theory of relativity is more staunchly defended than intelligent design. But not long ago, Einstein’s theory was argued against due to its peculiarity. I will neither make nor require that a position of truth be maintained as far as scientific culture goes; on account of it being treated as a feature of culture. But there are, like aesthetic movements, scientific movements that fight resistance. The impetus is not beauty or inspiration but rather whether or not the theory fits empirical observation. When the empirical evidence is overwhelming or compelling it might provide enough momentum to drive a scientific movement or even start a new scientific age. But I will again ignore the role of unique people, and places, and events in this process. These being important catalysts. 
	I want to return to a problem that I mentioned. What if some, or even most people, even in the same greater culture, accept competing or contradictory scientific theories? How do we determine what the scientific culture should be? In fact, because we live in such a volatile time, there is really no reason why a person needs to commit to one scientific theory over another. There likely will be entirely new options that will present themselves in the not-so-distant future. One way to unite us in an intellectualized, rather than aestheticized, culture is to rely on a non-epistemic standard. Interestingly, false ideas and theories, if consistent, can be instantiated in the mind just as easily as true ones. For instance, there is no crime in instantiating Aristotle's theory of essentialism or Lamarckian acquired characteristics in the mind, and even understanding such theories, as opposed to Darwinian natural selection. You need not consent to such theories. But then again you need not consent to true theories either. And this is why the extreme skeptic isn’t necessarily a lunatic. Actually, you would be doing yourself a great disservice if you were unaware of Aristotle and Lamarck. 
	We might then extend this to include a very large number of ideas or theories. If we make this adjustment, we can avoid the contradictions mentioned regarding scientific culture. Thus, in a sense, we might point to a more general kind of culture, an idea culture, which I would call the Collective Conscious. This would include all ideas, true or false, instantiated by most or all humans which they are conscious of. Because all ideas are the same, there will be an identical collection of ideas shared and instantiated at the same time, i.e., by the human species. Because both true and false ideas can be instantiated, this collection would tend to grow larger over time and become more complex. Of course, unlike the Collective Unconscious and archetypes, the Collective Conscious is not a permanent feature of the brain, and is fickle. For instance, if a dark age were to occur there would likely be a regression in the total content of the Collective Conscious. So, while the cultural trajectory points toward continuous growth, it is not guaranteed. 
	In ending with something uplifting, the Collective Conscious is likely the reason humans were able to conquer the planet and advance as quickly as we did. Mass coordination on the scale we’ve seen would not have been possible without it, whether it be socially or technologically. And the fast trail of history gives credence to this fact. Interestingly, and contrarily, the Collective Unconscious actually tends to favor and promote stagnation. And that is likely why we were stuck for a long time until consciousness and language were achieved. An interesting question is whether there is also another Collective-something emerging with the proto-archetypes. Maybe so? Though it would be much less stable than both the Collective Unconscious and the Collective Conscious. 
